# Design Plan — Smartphone Battery (case t6_r2)

Objective: smartphone cell with volumetric ED ≥ 950 Wh/L; 4C fast charge without lithium plating; T_max ≤ 50 °C under 4C fast charge; anode SEI ≤ 500 nm after 100 cycles; discharge voltage plateau ≥ 4.1 V.

Objective decomposition with trade-off notes:
- ED_vol ≥ 950 Wh/L (contract formula, calc-energy): benefits from thick dense electrodes, thin separators/collectors, high cathode voltage. Conflicts with 4C plating (thick electrodes → mass transport limit → anode potential dip) and T_max (thick electrodes → more heat, poor dissipation).
- Plateau ≥ 4.1 V: NMC811/graphite cell discharge midpoint ≈ 3.6 V → ceiling ~3.8 V with utilization tricks; 4.1 V requires high-voltage cathode chemistry (LNMO spinel, 4.7 V-class, cell midpoint ≈ 4.17 V per in-skill anchor). → Expected: ceiling escalation to LNMO system switch (ceiling_escalation ON).
- 4C no plating: negative surface potential ≥ 0 V vs Li/Li+ throughout 4C charge at 45 °C. Levers: N/P ratio, negative particle size (smaller → higher surface area), electrolyte conductivity/diffusivity (σ↑, D↑), thinner electrodes, higher porosity.
- T_max ≤ 50 °C (323.15 K) under 4C charge at 45 °C ambient (318.15 K): ΔT budget = 5 K. Levers: cooling coefficient h (thermal management DOF), DCR reduction (thinner electrodes, higher σ), lower charge heat (less polarization).
- SEI ≤ 500 nm after 100 cycles: graphite anode SEI growth under ec-reaction-limited model. Baseline magnitude ~450 nm (signal-scale reference from protocol: Chen2020 kinetics ×0.1 → 449→385 nm). Levers: SEI kinetic rate constant / exchange current density (coating), or accept baseline if ≤ 500 nm.

